  "title": "3Sum",
  "difficulty": "medium",
  "topics": ["array", "two pointers", "sorting"],
  "asked": "very_common",
  "statement": "Given a list of integers `nums`, return every set of three values that adds up to 0. The three values must come from three different positions in the list, and each distinct triplet should be reported only once, even if it can be formed in several ways (for example [-1, 0, 1] and [0, 1, -1] count as the same triplet).\n\nThe triplets may be returned in any order, and the numbers inside a triplet may be in any order. If no triplet adds up to 0, return an empty list.\n\nConstraints: 3 <= len(nums) <= 3000; -10^5 <= nums[i] <= 10^5.",
  "functionName": "three_sum",
  "signature": "def three_sum(nums: list[int]) -> list[list[int]]:",
  "starterCode": "def three_sum(nums: list[int]) -> list[list[int]]:\n    \"\"\"Return all distinct triplets of values from nums that add up to 0.\"\"\"\n    pass\n",
  "starterCodeJs": "/**\n * Return all distinct triplets of values from nums that add up to 0.\n * @param {number[]} nums\n * @return {number[][]}\n */\nfunction threeSum(nums) {\n\n}\n",
  "examples": [
    {"input": "nums = [-3, 1, 1, 2, -3, 0, -1]", "output": "[[-3, 1, 2], [-1, 0, 1]]", "explanation": "Two distinct triplets work: -3 + 1 + 2 and -1 + 0 + 1. The repeated -3s and 1s do not create extra copies."},
    {"input": "nums = [0, 0, 0, 0]", "output": "[[0, 0, 0]]", "explanation": "All four zeros are interchangeable, so the single triplet [0, 0, 0] is reported once."},
    {"input": "nums = [-2, 0, 0, 2, 2]", "output": "[[-2, 0, 2]]", "explanation": "Only the values -2, 0 and 2 can be combined, and the repeated 0 and 2 still give just one triplet."}
  ],
