  "compare": "unordered_nested",
  "referenceSolution": "def three_sum(nums: list[int]) -> list[list[int]]:\n    nums.sort()\n    triplets = []\n    for i in range(len(nums) - 2):\n        if nums[i] > 0:\n            break\n        if i > 0 and nums[i] == nums[i - 1]:\n            continue\n        lo, hi = i + 1, len(nums) - 1\n        while lo < hi:\n            total = nums[i] + nums[lo] + nums[hi]\n            if total < 0:\n                lo += 1\n            elif total > 0:\n                hi -= 1\n            else:\n                triplets.append([nums[i], nums[lo], nums[hi]])\n                lo += 1\n                while lo < hi and nums[lo] == nums[lo - 1]:\n                    lo += 1\n    return triplets\n",
  "targetComplexity": {"time": "O(n^2)", "space": "O(1) extra (ignoring the output and the sort)"},
  "hints": [
    "Fixing one of the three numbers turns the problem into a smaller one. What smaller problem is it?",
    "Checking every triple is O(n^3). If the list were sorted, how could two pointers find a pair with a given sum in one sweep?",
    "Sorting also helps with the 'no repeated triplets' rule. What do equal neighbours tell you about which values to skip, and where do you skip them?",
    "Try [0, 0, 0, 0] and [-5, -5, -5, 10, 10, 10]. How many triplets should come back, and does your skipping logic produce exactly that many?",
    "Sort, then for each distinct first value run a two-pointer sweep over the rest, skipping repeated values for the first number and for the second after each match."
  ],
  "followUps": [
    "What are the time and space complexity, and where does the O(n^2) come from?",
    "Why does sorting first make the duplicate handling easy, and what would you do without sorting?",
    "How would you change the code to count the triplets instead of listing them?",
    "How does this generalise to 4Sum or k-Sum, and what is the resulting complexity?"
  ],
  "idealAnswerPoints": [
    "Sorts the list, fixes the first value and uses two pointers on the rest for O(n^2) total",
    "Skips repeated first values and repeated second values after a match so each triplet appears once",
    "Moves the pointers by comparing the running total with zero",
    "States O(n^2) time and notes O(n^3) brute force or the hash-set variant as alternatives"
  ],
  "weakAnswerSigns": [
    "Tries all triples with three nested loops and de-duplicates with a set at the end",
    "Skips duplicates incorrectly and either misses [-5, -5, 10] or reports [0, 0, 0] many times",
    "Uses the same index twice or reuses an element within one triplet"
  ],
  "rubric": {
    "correctness": "Returns every distinct triplet exactly once, including repeated values like [-5, -5, 10], all-zero lists and lists with no answer.",
    "efficiency": "O(n^2) time with sorting and two pointers. A triple loop does not meet the target for n up to 3000.",
    "code_quality": "Early exit when the smallest value is positive, tidy duplicate skipping, and no leftover set/sort of results.",
    "communication": "Explains the sort + two-pointer idea and walks through how duplicates are skipped on a small example."
  }
